.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# ── Paper and research working files (excluded regardless of repo visibility) ─
# These stay out of git whether the repo is public or private. This is a
# separation-of-concerns decision, not only an exposure one.
#
# paper/  — the manuscript lives in Overleaf, which is its own source of truth
#   and its own version history. Mirroring it here creates two divergent copies
#   and no way to tell which is current. (Exposure matters too: this repo is
#   named in the paper, so a public copy carrying author names would break
#   double-blind review, and some venues treat it as a prior preprint.)
#
# docs/lit-review-final.md — internal audit. It records that the earlier
#   report's bibliography was fabricated, names a previously cited source as a
#   predatory-publisher proceeding, and logs the citation errors found. That is
#   the right thing to keep for ourselves and the wrong thing to ship in a
#   deliverable, private repo or not.
#
# Back these up outside git — Overleaf history for the paper, shared drive for
# the audit. Neither has a safety net here.
#
# NOTE: scripts/ migrations are NOT ignored — the paper states that migrations
# are publicly available, so they must stay in the repo.
paper/
docs/lit-review-final.md